<article class="env-lemma" id="0666">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0666">Lemma <span data-tag="0666">15.31.9</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. Let $I \subset J \subset A$ be ideals. Assume that $J/I \subset A/I$ is generated by an $H_1$-regular sequence. Then $I \cap J^2 = IJ$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      To prove this choose $g_1, \ldots , g_ m \in J$ whose images in $A/I$ form a $H_1$-regular sequence which generates $J/I$. In particular $J = I + (g_1, \ldots , g_ m)$. Suppose that $x \in I \cap J^2$. Because $x \in J^2$ can write 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  x = \sum a_{ij} g_ ig_ j + \sum a_ j g_ j + a  \]
</div>
    <p>
       with $a_{ij} \in A$, $a_ j \in I$ and $a \in I^2$. Then $\sum a_{ij}g_ ig_ j \in I \cap (g_1, \ldots , g_ m)$ hence by Lemma <a href="/tag/0665" data-tag="0665">15.31.8</a> we see that $\sum a_{ij}g_ ig_ j \in I(g_1, \ldots , g_ m)$. Thus $x \in IJ$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>